new file mode 100644
index 0000000..2c1ad27
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/BUILD.md
@@ -0,0 +1,123 @@
+# Building the badge firmware
+
+How the custom badge-menu firmware is built, what the build produces, and the execution order of
+both the build and the firmware itself.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Prerequisites
+
+- **Toolchain — JieLi pi32v2.** The firmware is built with JieLi's `pi32v2` cross clang. The build
+  expects it at `/opt/jieli/pi32v2/bin` (`clang`, `lto-wrapper`, `lto-ar`, `objcopy`, `objdump`),
+  with `../lib/r3-large` and `../include` alongside. Put it on the `PATH`:
+
+  ```sh
+  export PATH="/opt/jieli/pi32v2/bin:$PATH"
+  ```
+
+- **Native post-build tools (Linux).** Turning the linked ELF into a flashable image is done by the
+  native Linux tools vendored in `SDK/tools/linux/` (`isd_download`, `ufw_maker`, `fw_add`,
+  `packres`, `json_to_res`, `fat_comm`, `remove_tailing_zeros`). The Makefile points `POST_TOOL_DIR`
+  at them; you do not invoke them by hand.
+
+- **Open-file limit (`ulimit -n`).** The final link opens **all** objects and libraries at once
+  (~1130 files). If your shell has a low limit (1024 is a common default), `ld` runs out of
+  descriptors partway through and the link fails with a **misleading** message —
+  `file not recognized: File format not recognized` on some arbitrary `.o` (the file is fine; what
+  fails is the `open()`, and BFD reports it as if the format were invalid). Raise it before
+  building:
+
+  ```sh
+  ulimit -n 65536
+  ```
+
+  Check the current one with `ulimit -n` (and the allowed maximum with `ulimit -Hn`). To make it
+  permanent, put it in your `~/.bashrc`. Note: `env -i` does **not** reset `ulimit`s (it only clears
+  environment variables), so a "clean" environment still carries this limit.
+
+- **Chipkey (optional, per unit).** `SDK/cpu/br35/tools/download/watch/chipkey_<id>.bin` is the
+  device's flash key, selected with `BADGE_CHIPKEY=<id>`. It is device-specific and is not committed
+  — provide your own. Without it the build completes with a keyless image.
+
+## 2. Build
+
+```sh
+cd SDK
+ulimit -n 65536        # the link opens ~1130 files at once (see §1)
+make clean
+BADGE_CHIPKEY=9847 make MENU=1
+```
+
+`MENU=1` adds `-DCONFIG_BADGE_MENU` to the compiler defines. That single define selects the
+**badge-menu application** (LVGL touch menu) instead of the factory watch boot test. Outputs land in
+`SDK/cpu/br35/tools/` and `SDK/output/`.
+
+## 3. Build execution order
+
+All paths relative to `SDK/` (make runs there).
+
+1. **`make MENU=1` → root `Makefile`.** Sets up the Linux toolchain
+   (`TOOL_DIR=/opt/jieli/pi32v2/bin`, `CC=clang`, `LD=lto-wrapper`, `AR=lto-ar`,
+   `SYS_LIB_DIR`/`SYS_INC_DIR`), the post plumbing (`POST_SCRIPT=cpu/br35/tools/download.sh`,
+   `POST_TOOL_DIR=tools/linux`), the `CFLAGS` (`-target pi32v2 -mcpu=r3 -flto -Oz`) and the
+   `DEFINES`. `ifdef MENU` adds `-DCONFIG_BADGE_MENU`.
+
+2. **`pre_build`.** Preprocesses the generators with `clang -E -P` to produce the derived files:
+   the source list (`build/fileList.mk` from `build/fileList.c`/`genFileList.c`), the linker script
+   (`cpu/br35/sdk.ld` from `cpu/br35/sdk_ld.c`), the post script (`cpu/br35/tools/download.sh` from
+   `download.c`), and `isd_config.ini`. These generated files are not committed — they are
+   regenerated every time.
+
+3. **Compilation.** Each source in the list → `objs/<path>.c.o` via clang.
+
+4. **Link.** `lto-wrapper` links all objects with `-Tcpu/br35/sdk.ld` into
+   `cpu/br35/tools/sdk.elf`.
+
+5. **Post (`download.sh`).** `objcopy` extracts the ELF sections (`.text`, `.data`, `.data_code`,
+   overlays…) into raw `.bin` files and concatenates them into **`app.bin`**. Then `isd_download`
+   packages `app.bin` + uboot + resources + config (with the `chipkey_<id>.bin` key if present) into
+   the full flash image **`jl_isd.bin`** and the update image **`update.ufw`**. The images are copied
+   to `SDK/output/`.
+
+**Outputs**
+
+| File | What |
+|---|---|
+| `cpu/br35/tools/app.bin` | The raw application image (concatenated ELF sections) |
+| `cpu/br35/tools/jl_isd.bin` / `.fw` | Full flash image (uboot + app + resources + config) for USB flashing |
+| `cpu/br35/tools/update.ufw` | Native update image (USB flashing). **Not** usable as-is for BLE OTA — see the note in the [README](../README.md#quick-start) |
+
+## 4. Firmware execution order
+
+What runs on the device, from power-on to the badge menu (file:line in this tree).
+
+1. **Power-on → C runtime → `app_main()`** — `apps/watch/app_main.c`. Creates the `app_core` task
+   and calls `os_start()`.
+
+2. **`app_core` init.** Parses the config, initializes the key driver, runs the init-call chains
+   (`do_initcall` / `do_module_initcall` / `do_late_initcall`), `dev_manager_init`, and the
+   power-on/charge dispatch.
+
+3. **UI selection.** `app_main.c` calls `lvgl_ui_init(&ui_cfg_data)` when `CONFIG_LVGL_UI_ENABLE` is
+   active. Under `CONFIG_BADGE_MENU`, `apps/watch/board/br35/sdk_config.h` forces
+   `CONFIG_LVGL_UI_ENABLE = 1` (and `CONFIG_JL_UI_ENABLE = 0`) — so `MENU=1` is what turns on the
+   LVGL path.
+
+4. **`lvgl_ui_init()` → the `"ui"` task** — `apps/common/ui/lvgl_v8/lvgl_main.c` (`lvgl_main_task`).
+
+5. **`lvgl_main_task()`.** `lv_init` → `lv_port_disp_init` → `lv_port_indev_init` (AXS5106L touch),
+   then `jl_gui_init()`.
+
+6. **`jl_gui_init()`.** Under `CONFIG_BADGE_MENU` it builds the screens with `badge_menu_build()`
+   (menu / lock / mouse / scanner / spoof / update) and enters `badge_lock_enter()` — the device
+   boots **suspended** (display + touch off, black).
+
+7. **Badge main loop.** `lvgl_main_task` runs a periodic `lv_timer_handler()` loop and polls the
+   physical button value: while locked, any key runs `badge_lock_wake()`; on the lock image button 1
+   suspends and button 2 opens the menu; in the menu button 1 locks. The touch callbacks switch
+   scene with `lv_scr_load` (Mouse, Scanner, Update, Settings). BLE is off at boot and only turns on
+   in the Mouse and Update scenes.
+
+**`MENU=1` summary:** `-DCONFIG_BADGE_MENU` → `sdk_config.h` sets `CONFIG_LVGL_UI_ENABLE=1` →
+`app_main.c` calls `lvgl_ui_init()` → `lvgl_main_task` → `jl_gui_init()` selects `badge_menu_build()`
++ `badge_lock_enter()` instead of the factory watch app.
